Blueprint update validation
===========================

The feature is opt-in (--enable-blueprint-update). See
../concepts/blueprint_updates for the supported ordinary-object scope,
initializer restrictions, request limits and operator workflow. Interactive
objects remain rejected; these tests do not establish player-session support.

Reproduction
------------

Run the ordinary DEBUG suite from a clean configured build:

    cd src
    ./configure --enable-debug --enable-blueprint-update --enable-swap-efun
    make clean
    make
    cd ../test
    ./run.sh
    ./run.sh -v t-blueprint-update

Enable optional packages, including Python, when testing their integration.
Run the exhaustive allocation controls in a separate DEBUG build configured
with EXTRA_CFLAGS=-DBLUEPRINT_UPDATE_TESTING. Then run:

    ./run.sh t-blueprint-failures.sh t-blueprint-swap.sh t-blueprint-python \
        t-blueprint-retirement t-blueprint-workflow t-blueprint-classification

The ordinary build must report BLUEPRINT_PIPELINE_CONFIG: ordinary. The
instrumented build must report testing and positive failure/recovery counts
for all three pipeline modes. Disabled-feature skips are not enabled coverage.
The CI workflow retains required gcc/clang, i386/amd64 and enabled/disabled
jobs, with a separate required instrumented Python allocation job. Coverage
reporting jobs remain optional. Hosted CI results are a separate delivery gate.

For bounded pause and memory observations, run test/blueprint_stress.py with
its supplied cases.json. See test/blueprint_stress/README for exact commands,
optional Python requirements, metric definitions and evidence checks. These
scenarios are explicit and are not discovered by the ordinary t-* runner.
The native observer exists only in DEBUG/BLUEPRINT_UPDATE_TESTING builds.

Local release checks (2026-09-10)
--------------------------------

The final implementation passed the full 94-suite ordinary DEBUG run with
GCC 13 and PCRE, without Python. Five clean clang configurations passed 58
focused suite invocations: disabled, i386, Python, system allocator and
nonDEBUG. Four builds were warning-free; system malloc retained its fourteen
existing allocator warnings and does not provide native garbage collection.
A separate clean clang DEBUG/Python/PCRE instrumented build was warning-free.

The instrumented native pipeline covers all 419 path/explicit, 412 path/all
and 279 loaded/explicit failed allocation points, followed by successful
recovery. It also passes 222 swap checks, 346 retirement checks, the complete
workflow and 2604 Python handle checks. The Python sweep covers ten admission
and 299 execution failures, including both binding MemoryErrors. Ordinary
nonDEBUG workflows exercise actual updates without the private test controls.

The disposable NPC family passes 193 checks with the default two-second
alarm on ordinary GCC, native and all four enabled cross configurations.
It covers mixed historical generations, retained values/references/aliases,
inventory, heartbeats, queued callouts, successive updates, atomic rejection
and recovery, and absence of constructor/reset/initializer replay.

Memcheck qualification
----------------------

The final workflow and NPC-family runs have zero Memcheck errors and zero
definite/indirect loss. They retain 1080 and 896 possibly-lost bytes respectively.
The broader update run completes its LPC assertions but exits unsuccessfully
under Memcheck: 22 uninitialized write-buffer errors in three swap-writer
contexts, zero definite/indirect loss and 552 possibly-lost bytes. Earlier
broad-update evidence has the same diagnostic class in two contexts; the
third current context flushes through a different stdio path and is retained
as a distinct observation. The unchanged ordinary swap test reproduces four
write-buffer errors both with the feature enabled and with it disabled in a
matching GCC/Valgrind build, without any blueprint-update call. This is a
qualified existing swap-serialization issue, not a zero-error memory claim.
No custom suppression was added. Legacy blocker reference-count diagnostics
also match the previously recorded ordinary baseline exactly.

Bounded measurements
--------------------

All twelve supplied scenarios passed on an AMD Ryzen 7 8845HS, x86_64,
clang 18.1.3, DEBUG, Python, PCRE, Valgrind allocator redzones of four words,
and BLUEPRINT_UPDATE_TESTING. The benchmark itself ran without Memcheck,
with the normal two-second alarm and no concurrent validation jobs.
The measured binary SHA256 is:
  e9ae1df3209303a990c60695a5807f22befd8f68312d330cf845da3d19c126f3

These are individual local observations, not latency guarantees. Request
pause includes compilation through retirement; queue wait and result polling
are excluded. Batch pause also includes the surrounding request loop and
report expiry. Driver-accounted memory is sampled, not a transient compiler
peak or whole-process RSS. The README documents the wall-clock limitations.

  Clones  Retained vars  Handles  Request ms  Outcome
       1              8  none          0.395  completed
     100              8  none          0.693  completed
    1000              8  none          4.442  completed
   10000              8  none         48.214  completed
    1000             64  none         18.697  completed
     100            512  none         40.559  completed
     950            512  none         65.083  schema/work rejection
     100              8  Python        2.383  completed (first update)
     500              8  Python        5.928  runtime-work rejection

For 10000 clones, the batch took 48.229 ms; candidate total_size was 1688
bytes. At fully prepared storage, xalloc_used exceeded request entry by
2829552 bytes and xalloc_allocated by 3888384 bytes. A clone added after
admission raised the count to 10001 and was rejected before publication.
A four-request batch with 1000 native-handle clones took at most 27.174 ms
across two updates. Every rejected main cohort retained its original values
and programs; the independent source-only requests still completed.

Two native handles per clone plus their arrays/bindings added 687768
xalloc_used bytes for 1000 clones. For 100 Python-held clone pairs, driver
allocation increased by 20000 bytes; shallow wrappers totalled 16000 bytes,
and Python tracemalloc's current delta was 40568 bytes. These quantities
cover different allocation domains and are not interchangeable. Releasing
two newly named handles in each of ten generations retained 19984-20024
additional driver-accounted bytes per 100-clone cohort each time, measured
within a single LPC evaluation. Inactive declaration keys remain until
object disposal; this does not imply retention of the old programs.

Clone count alone is not an operating limit. Variable width, handle inventory,
prior generations, diagnostic work and memory can exhaust a different bound
first. Keep the documented limits and measure representative local families
before choosing an operational cohort size. No live MUD deployment or hosted
CI acceptance is inferred from this local validation.
